.TH BOSWAS-BOOTSPLASH 1 "November 2026" "boswas-bootsplash 1.0~alpha3" "Boswas OS"
.SH NAME
boswas-bootsplash \- control the Boswas OS boot screen
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boswas-bootsplash
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENT ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas-bootsplash
talks to the Boswas OS boot screen daemon,
.BR boswas-bootsplashd (8),
over its control socket. It is used by the initramfs scripts and the systemd
units of the package, and can be used by an administrator. Only root can
reach the daemon.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ping
Succeed if the boot screen is running.
.TP
.BR ask-password " [" \-\-prompt " \fITEXT\fR]"
Show a passphrase prompt on the boot screen, wait until the user presses
Enter, and write the passphrase to standard output without a trailing
newline. The characters typed are never shown; one dot per character is
drawn, up to 24. A prompt of the form
.RI \(dqPlease\ unlock\ disk\  NAME \(dq
(what the disk-unlock scripts ask) is shown as the title \(dqUnlock your
disk\(dq with
.I NAME
under it; any other text is shown as it is, under the title \(dqEnter your
passphrase\(dq.
.TP
.BR message " [\fITEXT\fR]"
Show one line of text under the ring of the boot screen (under the text box
while a prompt is open). It goes away after six seconds. A message that
reports a failure is shown in the danger colour and stays for thirty seconds,
or until the next passphrase is entered; one that reports a rejected
passphrase is shown as the sentence \(dqThat passphrase did not work. Try
again.\(dq, and if a prompt is open, or the next one opens while the message
is still shown, its text box shakes briefly. Without text, the line is
cleared.
.TP
.BI progress " PERCENT"
Report boot progress, 0 to 100. It is shown as a percentage on the message
line under the ring.
.TP
.BR mode " " boot | shutdown | reboot
Switch between the boot, shutdown and restart screens.
.TP
.B hide
Give the display back to the text console. The Escape key does the same.
.TP
.B show
Take the display again.
.TP
.BR quit " [" \-\-retain [=\fISECONDS\fR]]
End the boot screen and restore the text console. With
.BR \-\-retain ,
the last frame stays on the display until the next program (the login
screen) shows its own picture, for at most
.I SECONDS
(default 6); if nothing takes the display in that time, the text console
returns.
.TP
.BI new-root " DIRECTORY"
Tell the daemon that the system is moving from the initramfs to its real
root file system at
.IR DIRECTORY .
.TP
.B agent
Answer systemd's password requests
.RI ( /run/systemd/ask-password )
on the boot screen until the boot screen ends. Started by
.IR boswas-bootsplash-ask-password.service .
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-version
Print a summary of the commands, or the version.
.SH PLYMOUTH COMPATIBILITY
Inside the initramfs the program is also installed as
.IR /bin/plymouth ,
because Debian's disk-unlock helper
.RI ( /lib/cryptsetup/askpass )
and the initramfs scripts call that name. Started as
.BR plymouth ,
it accepts the commands those callers use:
.BR \-\-ping ,
.BR "ask-for-password \-\-prompt" ,
.BR "message \-\-text" ,
.BR "display-message \-\-text" ,
.BR "hide-message" ,
.BR hide-splash ,
.BR show-splash ,
.B quit
.RB [ \-\-retain-splash ],
and
.BR "update-root-fs \-\-new-root-dir" .
Other Plymouth commands that scripts issue for progress reporting are
accepted and ignored. It is not a general replacement for
.BR plymouth (1),
and no file named plymouth is installed on the root file system.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command was carried out.
.TP
.B 1
The boot screen is not running, or it refused the command.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was not understood.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/boswas-bootsplash/socket
The daemon's control socket (root only).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boswas-bootsplashd (8)
